"Local history of Ethiopia : Oaf Goja Siyon - Ozentari (https://nai.uu.se/download/18.39fca04516faedec8b248e22/1580829013451/ORTO05.pdf)\nciteturn295search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.3 years ago; Crawled: 6.7 years ago; \n\nIn the lower portion of this river, the lacustral sediments have been cut by erosion. The area was not systematically prospected, and the presence of gold-bearing gravel cannot be excluded in such a large area without foregoing prospecting.\n\nA study in the Omo basin (Millosevich 1939) has indicated basalts in the lower Trap series and acid volcanics in upper parts of the series. In the Omo valley are found Pre-Cambrian gneiss, mica schists, and phyllites.\n\nThe Ministry of Mines undertook a geological survey in 1962, with the aim to explore outcrops of iron ore.\n\n[Mineral 1966]\n\nAmong traditional beliefs of people in the southwest, there is also one about an Ometi goddess or spirit of the river Omo, by name T'alahé. The crocodile was regarded as an incarnation of T'alahé.\n\n[Trimingham 1952]\n\n1700s  \nTato Shagi Sherocho, who ruled Käfa/Kaffa between about 1775 and 1795, is said to have extended his kingdom as far as the Omo in the south-east and nearly to the confluence of the Omo and Dincha rivers in the south.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLocal history of Ethiopia : Keda - Keltu Mesha (https://nai.uu.se/download/18.39fca04516faedec8b248e02/1580829012619/ORTKED05.pdf)\nciteturn295search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.3 years ago; Crawled: 6.7 years ago; \nKäfa expansion was reportedly continued by Tato Shagi Sherocho, who ruled between about 1775 and 1795. He is said to have extended his kingdom as far as the Omo River in the south-east and nearly to the confluence of the Omo and Dincha Rivers in the south.  \n[Pankhurst 1997 p 351]  \nOne of the refugee groups from Ennarya, the Minjo, succeeded in usurping power in Kaffa, where they established the celebrated Minjo dynasty, which dominated that country from the first half of the seventeenth century to 1897 when the Minjo dynasty itself was abolished by the victorious imperial Shewan soldiers.  \nThe power of a king of Kaffa was limited by the power of the councillors of state. The seven councillors, *mikrecho*, made and unmade kings. The political structure of Kaffa was composed of a series of hierarchically organized clans, at the apex of which stood the royal clan, the Minjo, and under it were the seven most important clans in the kingdom, and the seven councillors of the state were the representatives of the seven clans.  \nA king of Kaffa was subjected to all sorts of restrictions. No one was allowed to see him. He wore a veil and he sat behind a curtain. He could not put his feet on the ground, and cotton cloth was always placed where he desired to walk. [Mohammed 1994 p 91-92]\n\n1800s  \nThe state of Kaffa was a reservoir of slaves for the Gibe region. According to the reports--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSultanate of Aussa (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Aussa)\nciteturn295search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; The state religion of the kingdom was Sunni Islam. ... * Kadhafo Mahammad ibn Kadhafo 1749–1779 ... On 6 December 1889, Aussa signed a treaty with the Italians, agreeing to become a protectorate of Italy. ... Welde Giyorgis Aboye then invaded the sultanate and defeated Sultan Muhammad Hanfare at the battle of Arraddó in January 1896.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Journal of African American History (Re)Envisioning the African Diaspora in Low Country Georgia, 1750–1800 179 (https://karencookbell.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/jaah2.pdf)\nciteturn295search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.0 years ago; Crawled: 16.0 years ago; \nThe Journal of African American History (Re)Envisioning the African Diaspora in Low Country Georgia, 1750–1800 179\n\nFootnotes and references text in two columns.\n\nLeft column:\n- 28 Barry, *Le Royaume du Waalo, 1659–1859: Le Sénégal avant la conquête* (Paris, France, 1972), 190; Barry, *Senegambia and the Atlantic Slave Trade* (New York, 1998), 81–112; J. E. Inikori, “Underpopulation in Nineteenth Century West Africa: The Role of the Export Slave Trade,” in Christopher Fyfe, ed., *African Historical Demography* (Edinburgh, Scotland, 1981), 295–308. Establishing concrete demographic patterns for Waalo, Kajor, and Baol in the late 18th century is problematic since records are incomplete or non-existent for this period. However, the accounts of French and English officials who resided in the coastal regions combined with the extant studies of the slave trade’s impact on Senegambia provide a partial reconstruction of the population. Jean Gabriel Pelletan, *Account of Mémoire sur la colonie française du Sénégal avec quelque considérations historique et politique sur le traite des Nègres* (Paris, France, 1801), 14; Pelletan, former administrator and director of the Senegal Company reported the population in the village surrounding the fort of Podor at 2,000; Philip Curtin, *Economic Change in Pre-Colonial Africa: Supplementary Evidence* (Madison, WI, 1975), 36, estimates that the population of Kajor was 3,000 in 1763. This figure represents the population of Dakar Village. In 1764, the population was 21,000. This figure is limited to the cavalry force, which consisted of 1,000 men; and the infantry, which consisted of 20,000 men. The population of Baol is estimated at 3,000, which includes Lambai only, the capital city of Baol. Pierre Labarthe, *Voyage du Sénégal pendant les 1784 et 1785, d’après les mémoires de La Jaille* (Paris, France, 1802), 177. Labarthe, a high ranking French official, records that after the census of July 1779 the population of the “island of Senegal” was 3,018 persons. This figure included 383 Europeans. These figures suggest that the population of Waalo,